WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400
In this lesson, we're looking at one of the most consequential design decisions in the multi-agent system.

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:13.600
When a sub-agent hits an error, does it handle that error itself, or does it surface the failure to a coordinator?

3
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:19.000
The answer isn't obvious, and getting it wrong in either direction can cause real problems.

4
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.600
Let's map out the decision space.

5
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.600
Every sub-agent failure ends up in one of two places, and the architect decides which.

6
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.580
Local recovery means the sub-agent catches the failure, handles it internally, and returns to the

7
00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:35.738
coordinator as if nothing happened.

8
00:00:35.799 --> 00:00:42.537
Coordinator propagation means the sub-agent surfaces the failure, passes structured information

9
00:00:42.537 --> 00:00:45.729
upward, and lets the higher-level plan adapt.

10
00:00:45.799 --> 00:00:52.160
In between those two sites, the propagation boundary, the interface where the architect decides,

11
00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.333
deliberately, which failures cross up and which stay contained.

12
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:04.199
That boundary isn't a runtime behavior, it's a design choice, and it should be documented, tested, and enforced.

13
00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:10.716
Most production failures in multi-agent systems trace back to a propagation boundary that was never

14
00:01:10.716 --> 00:01:14.534
consciously set and defaulted to one extreme or the other.

15
00:01:14.599 --> 00:01:20.400
Teams that skip a deliberate policy land on one of two extremes, and both have a name,

16
00:01:20.599 --> 00:01:26.599
Silent corruption happens when a sub-agent catches every exception and returns a vague success.

17
00:01:26.599 --> 00:01:32.028
The coordinator proceeds on the assumption that the task completed correctly, but the data it's

18
00:01:32.028 --> 00:01:35.342
planning on may be malformed, incomplete, or simply wrong,

19
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:41.400
and the defect hides until it causes downstream failures that are far harder to diagnose.

20
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.800
Coordinator thrash is the opposite.

21
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:50.466
Every internal hiccup propagates. A rate limit pause, a minor validation issue, a transient timeout,

22
00:01:50.466 --> 00:01:52.333
all flow up as error events.

23
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:59.400
The coordinator retries and escalates constantly, which masks the signal from the failures it should be acting on.

24
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.800
Neither extreme is a strategy. What you want is a deliberate boundary between them.

25
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.199
So what belongs at the local recovery level?

26
00:02:07.199 --> 00:02:10.399
Retryable transients are the clearest case.

27
00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:16.899
A retryable error is one where repeating the same call without changing anything might succeed.

28
00:02:16.899 --> 00:02:23.800
If the sub-agent hits a rate limit and waits 200 milliseconds before going through, the coordinator doesn't need to know.

29
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:28.699
That blip resolved itself. Recoverable validation failures are similar.

30
00:02:28.699 --> 00:02:33.699
If the agent receives malformed input, it can sanitize and reprocess.

31
00:02:33.699 --> 00:02:37.800
That's an internal correction, not a system-level failure.

32
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.500
Idempotent refetches fall in the same category.

33
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:46.199
The key test is whether the coordinator's plan depends on knowing this happened.

34
00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:52.643
If handling it locally leaves the coordinator with accurate information and a valid path forward,

35
00:02:52.643 --> 00:02:55.433
local recovery is the right design choice.

36
00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:01.600
On the other side of the boundary, some failures carry information the coordinator's plan genuinely depends on.

37
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.500
Scope violations are the clearest example.

38
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:10.600
If a sub-agent is asked to perform an action outside its authorization, that's not a transient blip,

39
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.199
and the coordinator's task decomposition may need to change entirely.

40
00:03:14.199 --> 00:03:20.699
Policy denials fall in the same category because a guardrail blocked the action permanently, and no

41
00:03:20.699 --> 00:03:22.734
retry will change that outcome.

42
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:29.014
Those are non-retryable errors. Repeating the same call produces the same failure, so every

43
00:03:29.014 --> 00:03:31.131
sub-agent needs a retry budget.

44
00:03:31.199 --> 00:03:36.600
A maximum number of local attempts before it stops and hands the failure upward.

45
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:42.597
Once that budget is spent with no progress, the coordinator needs to know so it can reroute,

46
00:03:42.597 --> 00:03:45.334
substitute a different agent, or escalate.

47
00:03:45.399 --> 00:03:49.600
Here's a practical test you can apply to any failure mode in your system.

48
00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:54.600
Ask, would the coordinator's higher-level plan change if it knew this happened?

49
00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.699
If yes, propagate.

50
00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:04.699
The failure affects scope, sequencing, or resource allocation, and surfacing it lets the coordinator adapt correctly.

51
00:04:04.699 --> 00:04:07.199
If no, recover locally.

52
00:04:07.199 --> 00:04:13.095
The failure is fully resolved inside the sub-agent's own retry or sanitization loop, and the

53
00:04:13.095 --> 00:04:16.235
coordinator receives accurate results regardless.

54
00:04:16.299 --> 00:04:21.700
If you're genuinely unsure, default to propagating with a low severity signal.

55
00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.700
Visibility is almost always safer than silence.

56
00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:27.700
An operator can dismiss a low-priority warning.

57
00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.700
No one can act on a failure that was never reported.

58
00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:34.700
When in doubt, let it surface with a clear label attached.

59
00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:37.700
One principle often gets skipped.

60
00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:42.700
Even when a sub-agent recovers locally, that recovery should leave a structured trace.

61
00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:45.700
Logging at the boundary isn't optional.

62
00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:53.200
The sub-agent should record the failure type, the timestamp, and how many retries it took before returning success.

63
00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:58.200
Why? Because patterns in local recoveries are diagnostic signals.

64
00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:04.221
If a particular sub-agent is retrying three times on nearly every call, something upstream is

65
00:05:04.221 --> 00:05:07.135
degraded, and no one can see it without logs.

66
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:14.200
A silent local recovery with no trace is the first step toward the silent corruption we talked about earlier.

67
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:19.932
The coordinator may not need to act on every recovery event, but the operator always needs

68
00:05:19.932 --> 00:05:24.136
visibility into how the system is actually behaving in production.

69
00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:29.200
The propagation boundary is a design decision, not a default.

70
00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:35.200
A deliberate policy prevents both silent corruption and coordinator thrash.

71
00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:39.200
Ask whether the coordinator's plan would actually change.

72
00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:46.200
Log transient recoveries local, propagate scope violations, policy denials, and exhausted retry budgets,

73
00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:53.200
and log every local recovery so operators can see the pattern before it becomes an incident.

74
00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.200
That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

